  "name": null,
  "problem": "The function $g$ is defined as\n$g(x)={\\int }_{-7}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+6x-7}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that, to calculate $g(-7),$ the lower and upper limits are the same. So, the integral evaluates to zero:\n\n$$\ng(-7)={\\int }_{-7}^{-7}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\n$$\n\nSo, plugging in $x=-7$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+6x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{{g}^{\u2032}(x)}{[{x}^{2}+6x-7]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -7}\\,\\frac{{g}^{\u2032}(x)}{2x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{g}^{\u2032}(-7)}{2\\cdot (-7)+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(-7)}{-14+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
